WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:13.826
Today's featured presenter is Lynda Smith.

00:00:13.826 --> 00:00:17.594
Lynda Smith brings over 30 years of
experience in marketing, sales and

00:00:17.594 --> 00:00:21.849
general management across a diverse set
of industries, and has spent the last 20

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:26.325
years working in the area of
high-technology products and services.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:30.763
In her capacity as an SVP marketing,
chief marketing officer for

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:36.171
a number of companies, including
Genesys Telecommunication Laboratories,

00:00:36.171 --> 00:00:40.687
Genpact, Jive, WeVideo, and
Twilio, Lynda has owned the full

00:00:40.687 --> 00:00:45.147
end-to-end marketing responsibility for
the organization.

00:00:45.147 --> 00:00:48.675
She has successfully driven product
marketing, product management,

00:00:48.675 --> 00:00:52.723
corporate marketing, field marketing,
demand creation, channel marketing,

00:00:52.723 --> 00:00:56.880
developer outreach, marketing research,
and strategy development.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.900
She's currently working as a consultant
and advisor for a variety of startups,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.950
ranging from social robotics to
cloud-based development services.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.120
She has experience in business
to business, consumer and

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.420
developer platform companies.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.740
Her global background is extensive, with
hands-on work with the US, Europe, India,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.295
China, and other major markets.

00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:22.050
Lynda has an undergraduate degree in
liberal arts from Simpson College, and

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.670
an MBA from Wharton School of Business
at the University of Pennsylvania.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.568
She's on the faculty of Stanford
University, where she serves as lecturer

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:36.026
in global entrepreneurial marketing
in the engineering school,

00:01:36.026 --> 00:01:41.260
and diverse programs for the Stanford
Center for Professional Development.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.690
Her passion is what is happening in
this next generation of marketing,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.670
where digital media and social
networks are reshaping the discipline.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.680
Now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Lynda.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.890
&gt;&gt; All right, great.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.860
Well, fantastic to be with all of you.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.360
And this is a really big topic.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.850
We're not going to be able to get
through every possible detail,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.460
but I really have a goal today
of being able to help you

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.960
see where this world of growth
hacking is really going.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.070
And so, we're going to go
through a number of things.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.482
I want to develop an understanding
of what growth hacking is,

00:02:15.482 --> 00:02:19.130
there's a big debate about is
growth hacking marketing or not.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.670
We're going to explore
how growth hacking is and

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.440
isn't like the traditional marketing
you may have experience with.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.360
And then,
I want you to gain some basics so

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.300
that you could start implementing
growth hacking in your business.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.420
Now, I'm going to tell you,

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.750
growth hacking has traditionally been
really focused on online businesses.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.020
It's natural there, it's a fit.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.970
You'll see that as we go through.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.370
But, I've had some experience now,
even working with a hardware company,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.670
where we've been able to take
principles of growth hacking,

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.830
to really drive revenue and
drive the business.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.220
So, want to give you some
perspectives on that as well.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.310
So, we're going to be looking at
growth hacking, how it's defined.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.990
The basics of growth hacking.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.920
We'll dive into your website,
because that's the heart and

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.830
soul of the growth hacking strategy.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.660
And then we'll get into process,
testing, and tools.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.700
So, the jury's out,
is it marketing or not?

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.220
And it's really funny, because I'll
get into conversations with people who

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.350
have other business cards,
how to grow, growth hacker, and

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.250
get this feeling from them of,
no it's not marketing.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.000
It's something totally different.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.250
Well, it is and it isn't.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.550
We're going to be talking about that.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:25.120
But I got to tell you, there have
been some great brands with great

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.910
growth stories that all really came
back down to how they implemented

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:34.640
these basic principles
around growth hacking.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.680
So, it started a while ago,
and people will say, well,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.920
how long has been this
been around in business?

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.850
How many years have people
been doing growth hacking?

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.640
Well, the reality is, it does kind
of date back all the way to 1996.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:50.600
Now, there was a company called Hotmail,
that you may or may not be familiar with.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.570
But they were really one of
the first people who implemented

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:58.600
some of the core principles that we now
see as a part of everyday growth hacking.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:03.070
And it really came down to someone
having to rethink how to do marketing

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:06.620
based on an absence of money to spend,
so they had to get super creative.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.550
Now, the way the Hotmail story goes down
is that they were working with their VCs

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.730
and one of the VCs suggested, well,

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:18.540
hey, why don't you use the product itself
to be able to get other people to jump on?

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.980
And so, they created a backlink.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:25.440
So essentially, every time you got an
email from someone with a Hotmail account,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.250
it enabled you to click on that link, go
back, and sign up for an account yourself.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.850
So, some might say that this
world of growth hacking

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.080
really started off back
with Hotmail in 1996.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.640
Probably in modern terms though,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.740
we really relate it back to
around the 2010 timeframe.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.860
That's when the term was really coined.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.030
And it was coined by a gentleman
by the name of Sean Ellis.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.700
And the reason he coined the term was,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.170
he had had some great start-up experience,
some great exits, and

00:04:55.170 --> 00:05:00.050
everyone saw what this guy was doing in
terms of marketing that felt so different.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:03.530
And so, they would hire him to
really help them get started on it.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.280
Come in and get them off the ground.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.045
Well, he didn't want
to be there full-time.

00:05:08.045 --> 00:05:10.670
It was time for
him to move on to the next venture, and so

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.850
he needed to hire someone
to step into his shoes.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.680
And he put out there a basic resume that
said, hey, looking for a VP of marketing,

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.710
looking for a chief marketing officer.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.610
And he wasn't finding the right skills
coming through when he would do

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:23.170
the interviews.

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:27.500
So he decided, all right, tired of doing
this, I'm going to come up with a new term

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:32.420
that better describes this individual,
that better describes what I need.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.520
Henceforth, the term growth
hacker was invented.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.060
And he went out with that resume, and
ended up getting the right type of people,

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.390
or the right type of job description,

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.450
and getting the right type
of people on top of that.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.210
So, what is Growth Hacking?

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.440
Well, it lives in this very
interesting intersection between

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.630
the traditional discipline of marketing
that we all know and product development.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:06:00.390
And when you look at the basic Wikipedia
term, which I think, a very strong

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:04.348
representation of this world of growth
hacking, basically says it's a process,

00:06:04.348 --> 00:06:09.550
it's data-driven, and
has a lot of rapid experimentation in it.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.980
And when you think about the difference
from the traditional world of marketing,

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:17.350
the traditional world of
marketing seems much more planned.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.740
Especially when you get into large
campaigns, the months, the weeks

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.440
that go into planning it, it's not about
that rapid experimentation, necessarily.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.070
So, I think when you really look into
growth hacking, it's this discipline that

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:33.253
lives at a very interesting intersection,
it heavily involves products, but

00:06:33.253 --> 00:06:37.930
it's data-driven, and it also has
this construct of rapid iteration.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.810
And we're going to drive a little
more into that as we proceed here.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.530
So, when you think about
the mission of a growth hacker,

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:49.350
it is about creating this
self-perpetuating machine.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:54.020
And this is largely tied to this
idea of using the product itself

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:55.580
to grow the target audience.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.115
And we'll talk about how that's done.

00:06:57.115 --> 00:07:01.340
Aaron from Everlane, StumbleUpon,
once again, one of the leaders in this

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:05.920
idea of growth hacking as a discipline,
saw great success from it.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.980
But he really is the one who
came back with this idea of,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.200
it's about self-perpetuation.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.810
It's about getting the product
itself to do the marketing for you.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:16.330
Having dug into this,

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:21.440
having used the principles myself, this is
how I look at it in terms of core tenets.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.050
So to start off,
it does have this deep tie in product.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:26.940
And when we're talking about
it in terms of implementation,

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.440
how people leverage the product to
do this, it's dependent on virality.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.500
going to go deeper into that as well.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.590
It lives in analytics.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.810
This is back to that
data-driven principle.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:41.370
It involves staying with the customer
across the entire funnel process.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.090
Now, I will say the trend
in marketing is for

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:49.610
marketers to actually be more involved
across the full customer lifecycle.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:53.540
But here in growth hacking, the growth
hacker, the leader, the head of the field

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:58.680
is like I own every aspect, because
I've gotta make sure that that product.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.430
Is doing what it needs to do
to actually drive that growth.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.750
It's a company culture,
and it's a mindset.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.240
Again, I'm going to give you some
more details on each of these.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.790
So let's start off with the product.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:14.690
Yes, product has always been a part
of the marketing discipline.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:17.750
And where I have been chief marketing
officer I have often had not only

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:21.070
product marketing but also product
management as a part of my team.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.470
But here growth hackers they have to
have the product they see themselves as

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.780
the people,
the person who owns the product, and

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.380
they see that product as that
cheap channel for growth,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:37.490
and they also see, optimizing
the product as a part of their job.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.740
So, they really take seriously
that they own that product,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.750
and when you talk about company culture,
you'll see how there's a really

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.050
interesting difference there in a lot of
the companies that apply growth hacking,

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.700
but product is central to
what they're trying to do.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.380
Virality, a little more on that.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.490
Getting your products out
there in such a way that it

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:02.130
spontaneously will drive growth
really becomes a big focus.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.630
The virality stems from it being
engineered into the product itself, so

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.520
this goes back to ideas
like the backlink and

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:13.530
we'll go through a whole list of these
in product tools that are actually used.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.690
So a growth hacker is looking every
day at how do I make this go viral?

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:22.940
And when you think about it,
just a few quick rules on virality.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:26.460
First of all, rule number one is make it
easy for others to spread your product.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:30.040
Back links, Hotmail,
we talked a little bit about that.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:34.190
It's also about provoking this
desire to spread the product.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:38.560
To have the product must be something that
everyone wants to share out, and then rule

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.130
number three is finding ways to do all of
this without spending very much money.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.600
So morality is very key to all of this,
and

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.880
again the reason why the growth
hacker feels they own the product,

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.630
if that product has to be pretty darn
special to create that notion of morality.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:01.230
Analytics, so the first day that a growth
hacker gets on the job may be different

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:02.770
than any other position that you see.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.790
He dives in, he or she dives in, and

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:12.060
really looks at what are the event based
analytics that I need to understand?

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.060
What's going on with the product?

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.148
How are people using it?

00:10:15.148 --> 00:10:16.860
How is sign-up going?

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.100
How are people going
through the sign-up flow?

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.010
And then they'll sit down with
anyone that touches somewhere where

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:23.540
they need analytics.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.450
And really look for,
how are we going to wire this product?

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.270
How are we going to wire the website?

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:32.900
So I get the analytics I need to
drive growth into the company.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:36.040
Part of the reason in analytics again,

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.790
very important across
all forms of marketing.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:39.800
But for the growth factor,

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.790
one it keeps them honest because he
really is going through and saying,

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:48.005
yeah let's look at where our success track
record is, what works, what didn't work.

00:10:48.005 --> 00:10:52.455
Let's keep what worked, let's not dive any
further into stuff that's not working.

00:10:52.455 --> 00:10:55.025
It allows the growth hackers
to stack right into the to do,

00:10:55.025 --> 00:10:57.065
because every day is
about experimentation.

00:10:57.065 --> 00:10:59.895
Every day, the growth hacker is waking up,
looking at the numbers and

00:10:59.895 --> 00:11:03.735
saying what are we going to try
today to initiate more growth?

00:11:03.735 --> 00:11:07.790
It makes success repeatable, because you
know what's worked and not worked and

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.810
it also allows you to
somewhat predict the future.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:14.140
So analytics, another one of
the key thrust in growth hacking.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.840
The funnel process again, in all
marketing disciplines we have a funnel,

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:22.640
we're trying to drive
people through that funnel.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.398
Here the growth hacker thinks
about owning all the steps.

00:11:25.398 --> 00:11:29.580
Acquisition, activation, retention,

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.150
revenue, and then that referral,
that referral that drives that virility.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.390
So keen interest in owning
everything that touches the funnel.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.310
Company culture, now this is the piece
that when I get into these discussions

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:45.630
with friends who say, yep, I'm the head
of growth, I'm a growth hacker.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.160
The thing that I have found
that is different about their

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.930
company from other companies is,
they are a power from the top down to own

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.630
anything that might get
in the way of growth.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:12:01.360
So, if you think about traditionally,
marketing in an organization is a pier

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.100
to other disciplines, the engineering
discipline, the customer support

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:09.040
discipline, the IT disciplines, G and A,
all the other disciplines in the company.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:13.350
And that had that CMO that had
a marketing is sitting in a table

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:16.470
with their peers supporting in to the CEO.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:20.660
What I found in the culture of companies
that are truly embracing this idea of

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:25.320
growth hacking is the CEO has essentially
told those peers, hey, you work for

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.940
me but you also work for
our head of growth.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:31.040
At any point in time, if our head of
growth says you need to change something,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.040
do something differently.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.580
You've got to get out of the way and
make it happen.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:36.690
Because that's the person
who owns the numbers.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.060
That's the person that
we've got to enable.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:44.530
So there's a different level of power
if you will for that head of growth.

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:48.010
Then you might find the head of
marketing having in other companies.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.760
And then as I said it's a mind set.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:55.980
You know, it really comes down to when
you talk to people who are passionate

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:58.790
about being a growth hacker,
what they think about is hey,

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:02.230
my job isn't marketing
as you might know it.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.750
My job is driving growth.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:07.720
My job is owning the revenue number or
the use number,

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.340
and it's about doing it really fast.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.490
Because growth has to move quickly.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:15.080
So there's a different mindset that you

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:19.050
might find in someone that's considered
themselves truly a growth hacker.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.250
If you're hiring for this head of growth,

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.320
there are a few things that you
really need to be looking for.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.620
This person has to be
passionate about data.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:32.350
They have to love waking up every
morning and living in the numbers, and

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:36.810
understanding how those numbers
are shifting and changing over time.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.350
They're super curious about everything.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:39.430
They're poking at everything.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:40.370
They're poking at the products.

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:41.900
They're poking at customer support.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:47.060
They're poking on anything that might
change where those numbers are going.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.720
They're infinitely creative.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.880
They want to try all sorts of stuff, throw
it against the wall, see what sticks.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:55.290
They're obsessive.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:58.960
When it comes to they tried something and
yeah it kind of worked but

00:13:58.960 --> 00:13:59.700
let's try it again.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:00.680
See if we can do it better.

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:04.970
They're constantly trying to be
