00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:39.850
Feeling a part of the company.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.640
Feeling a part of where things are going.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.590
Using things like newsletters to keep

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:49.230
people excited about being connected
into your product and your company.

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:52.420
Top customer programs, rewards, t-shirts.

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:56.180
Whatever your customer base wants,
having them be able to be a part of it,

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:58.045
rewarding them with a customer program.

00:27:59.055 --> 00:27:59.795
Building a community.

00:27:59.795 --> 00:28:04.415
Sometimes it's as simple as people being
able to leverage, and connect, and

00:28:04.415 --> 00:28:08.545
contact other people that are using
your product or your service, and

00:28:08.545 --> 00:28:12.904
giving them a community platform to
do that, and then making them stand.

00:28:12.904 --> 00:28:17.662
Again, one of my memories of Twilio is
walking down the street, people seeing

00:28:17.662 --> 00:28:22.863
a Twilio t-shirt, or my Twilio track
jacket, and saying my God, we love Twilio.

00:28:22.863 --> 00:28:24.495
Well boy, when you have raving fans,

00:28:24.495 --> 00:28:27.410
they're out there creating that
virality by word of mouth for you.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:29.520
On the organic side,

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:32.330
again, some of it's going to be very
familiar to traditional marketing.

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:36.210
The organic things that retain customers
are things like cost of switching.

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.640
If you've got a lot invested in
getting started, getting set up,

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.960
that's a high cost of switching and
likelihood is you're going to stay.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:46.450
Comfortable with the user experience,
wow, it's so easy to use.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.510
I just really can't think
about using something else and

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:50.794
then the network effects.

00:28:50.794 --> 00:28:54.280
Even if maybe the product is not as great,
as what someone would want,

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:57.390
the fact that everyone else is tiding
into it will help people retain.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:29:02.610
So those are your basics in terms of
getting people to the top of the funnel,

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:04.140
activating them and retaining them.

00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:08.510
And believe me, we can actually go
into all of those in much more detail.

00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:09.770
So that gives you the basics.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:13.530
So let's talk about your website.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:19.140
The reality is, the website is for
a grow packer the front door.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:24.499
It is how people find them, they activate
and even retained in the long run.

00:29:24.499 --> 00:29:27.743
So when you think about the web page,
what you want to think about is the top of

00:29:27.743 --> 00:29:30.430
the funnel,
it's really that persuasive end.

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:32.970
It's the home page,
it's products and services page,

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:35.000
it's basic information pages.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:39.520
It's all that content that sets
up the value proposition and

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:41.430
persuades people to take action.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:44.180
And then you think about
the transactional end.

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:47.940
Those pages on your website,
where the transaction actually occurs,

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:52.140
where that activation actually occurs,
those can be forms, those can be

00:29:52.140 --> 00:29:56.350
shopping carts, so, as you're looking
at your website, a new perspective.

00:29:56.350 --> 00:30:00.970
It's not just this very informational
source, it's not just this pretty source

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:04.750
It's really about this persuasive end,
my top of the funnel and

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:08.240
then transactional end that's
getting people to activate.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.660
Now going back to a difference from
a growth hacker's perspective,

00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:16.540
their thinking about how
do I engineer these pages

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:18.910
to drive more activations,
to drive more sign ups.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:23.780
It's not about is it nice looking, it's
not about is there great content just for

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:27.980
content sake, they're really
engineering what's going on.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:32.600
So a few best practices
to optimize your site and

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:34.960
get those activations you're looking for.

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:37.390
First of all, no distraction.

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:40.670
You don't want people coming to
the website like that's nice,

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:43.150
let me wander overt here,
let me look over here.

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:45.280
You want to get them to
what you want them to do.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.440
You want enough information that they
immediately go I got to sign up.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:52.257
Speed matters.
When it comes to websites,

00:30:52.257 --> 00:30:56.295
if it takes a long time for things to
load and we all get enamored by flash and

00:30:56.295 --> 00:31:00.220
design, if it takes a long time to load,
you're going to lose people.

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:04.830
When you have very short term memory, you
have needs for immediate satisfaction so

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:05.930
speed matters.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:09.100
People value social proof.

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:10.810
Get right out there on front.

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:11.810
Who else is doing it?

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:14.920
Who else like them is interested
in the service or in the product?

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:18.160
Think about every page as a home page.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:20.855
Wow, with all the things that
link now to our websites,

00:31:20.855 --> 00:31:22.960
someone may not hit that homepage.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:24.870
They may actually come to another page.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:27.730
So make sure that sign-up,
that activation,

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:30.870
call to action is there on every page.

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:32.000
And then think mobile.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:36.420
We are a mobile generation, we're a mobile
world, think mobile first because you may

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:39.620
actually see your activations
happening from a mobile channel.

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.960
I talked about a moment ago this
idea of CTA, call to action.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:49.180
Great calls to action,
give clear direction.

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:52.020
Don't make visitors figure
out what you want them to do.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:55.480
Don't get overly cute,
don't get overly creative.

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:59.990
Make sure that it's clear that you
want them to click here and sign up.

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:01.600
Make the CTA obvious.

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:03.110
The buttons need to stand out.

00:32:03.110 --> 00:32:06.315
And you'll laugh about, yeah, we spend
a lot of time, is it a blue button,

00:32:06.315 --> 00:32:07.980
is it a red button, is it a gray button?

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:11.341
The reason we spend time doing the A/B
testing on that is based on your target

00:32:11.341 --> 00:32:13.190
audience, that could all be different.

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:16.300
But make sure your button stands out for
your target audience.

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:18.060
And if you have a phone number involved,

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:22.000
make it prominent,
don't make people go digging for it.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:25.730
A few basics to how to make your growth
hacking website really stand out.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:29.621
Now, final bit, testing.

00:32:29.621 --> 00:32:32.750
I told you that the growth hacker is
constantly looking at the numbers.

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:37.650
The growth hacker is constantly looking
at what's working, what's not working.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:40.107
So what they're doing is
a lot of A/B testing.

00:32:40.107 --> 00:32:44.165
So for those of you who have not been
thrown into the world of A/B testing, and

00:32:44.165 --> 00:32:47.928
thank goodness we have some awesome
software now that helps us do this.

00:32:47.928 --> 00:32:51.762
The idea is you get visitors to
come in to your websites, but

00:32:51.762 --> 00:32:56.606
through automation what you've been
able to do is key up a number of tests.

00:32:56.606 --> 00:32:59.798
So you can have a person come in,
and what they see on your website is

00:32:59.798 --> 00:33:03.450
the control page, the standard
page that you've had up there.

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:05.830
But someone else coming in
gets served up variation A,

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:11.340
while another one gets served up variation
B, and you can look at what's working.

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:13.140
Are they hitting the buttons
I want them to hit?

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:14.370
Are they activating?

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:15.880
Are they doing what I want them to do?

00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:21.120
And with that information, you're able
to go back and revamp what you've got.

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:24.160
What do you want to A/B test?

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:25.110
Everything.

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:27.870
It can be the product itself
that you're A/B testing.

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:29.150
But it's your call to action,

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:32.640
it's the content, it's the copy,
it's the visual, it's the navigation.

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:35.210
It's those forms that
are the bottom of the funnel.

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:38.360
It's ads, it's emails, it's pricing.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:43.270
Again, if you're a growth hacker, every
day you're living to try something new.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:46.340
See what works, keep what works,
and throw away what doesn't.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:50.090
And as I said, they have some great

00:33:50.090 --> 00:33:53.420
tools that we can use to be able
to do this constant A/B testing.

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:55.810
And you'll find there
are a number of them out there,

00:33:55.810 --> 00:33:57.829
Optimizely is one that I personally use.

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:02.550
But basically, each of these tools does
a great job of splitting that traffic.

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:06.020
It sets that control mechanism
to ensure each visitor

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.410
has a consistent experience
rather than a random one.

00:34:08.410 --> 00:34:11.220
It tracks the goals of
the conversion rates for

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:14.980
each variation, gets you some great tests,
shows the statistical data,

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:18.640
so that every day,
you can try something new.

00:34:18.640 --> 00:34:21.850
And then a final comment on tools,
analytics.

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:27.692
Think all of us as marketers have gotten
very familiar with general analytics,

00:34:27.692 --> 00:34:31.019
things that Google
actually provides to us.

00:34:31.019 --> 00:34:34.867
In addition to that, every growth hacker's
going to dive into event based and

00:34:34.867 --> 00:34:36.750
people based analytic tools.

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:41.160
Because they have to understand what's
going on through that website based on

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:46.630
events, based on people's actions,
not to mention, also inside that product.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:52.270
So, 45 minutes,
I know we want to leave time for Q and A.

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:57.900
That has given you the basic role,
if you will, of what growth hacking is.

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.240
How you implement a growth
hacking strategy,

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.880
how that is like and
dislike what's going on in marketing.

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:06.799
And then, looking at process and tools.

00:35:09.331 --> 00:35:12.138
All right, let's jump into some questions.

00:35:12.138 --> 00:35:15.355
&gt;&gt; So the first question,
Lynda that we got submitted is,

00:35:15.355 --> 00:35:20.110
where has growth hacking been used for
services opposed to products?

00:35:20.110 --> 00:35:24.679
And what is the most significant
difference in applying growth

00:35:24.679 --> 00:35:26.325
hacking to services?

00:35:26.325 --> 00:35:27.460
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:30.670
Let me start off by answering it in terms

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:34.470
of the online world versus
the more traditional world.

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:38.870
So when you think about growth hacking,
everything I just walked you through,

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:43.105
thinking about the website,
it really has us bent to products and

00:35:43.105 --> 00:35:46.600
services that are actually online.

00:35:46.600 --> 00:35:51.140
What I have found is that there's some
elements of this that we can really draw

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:55.418
into services and products that
are offered in an offline world.

00:35:55.418 --> 00:36:00.240
It things like really thinking about
that whole life cycle of a customer and

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:02.570
how marketing can be really engaged in it.

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:06.110
It's about looking at analytics, but
looking at them on a more daily basis,

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:09.570
so that's it's not just a once
a month type of report.

00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:13.230
It's looking at things like
diving deep into my product.

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:15.460
Is my product what my market needs?

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:18.905
And these are all very traditional
things we look as marketing.

00:36:18.905 --> 00:36:22.121
But when you're thinking about it
in this growth hacking mentality,

00:36:22.121 --> 00:36:25.020
there's this rapid fire
experimentation that comes into it.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:29.200
Now in terms of services, when you
think about products and services.

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.340
When you think about services,

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:34.670
I think about something like
DropBox actually as a service.

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:36.180
It's a storage service.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:40.520
There's a product that's a part of it,
but the reality is, to me as a consumer,

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:43.280
it's a service, it's my storage service.

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:47.170
I think it's really diving into
what your type of service is.

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:50.700
And in fact, can you think about your
service a little bit differently?

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:52.384
I hope that answers that.

00:36:52.384 --> 00:36:54.400
&gt;&gt; I think that's great.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:57.500
The next question we have here is,
can you give some examples of

00:36:57.500 --> 00:37:01.928
recent companies that successfully used
growth hacking and how they did it?

00:37:01.928 --> 00:37:06.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah well, the lot of them are ones
that you're very, very familiar with.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:11.580
It really comes down to any of the social
platforms that we have participated in.

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:16.344
You will find that they are using
growth hacking as a large part of how

00:37:16.344 --> 00:37:18.110
they look at marketing.

00:37:18.110 --> 00:37:20.880
And they're using a lot of
the end product tactics.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:25.130
But they're also constantly every day
driving in to looking at the product.

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:29.850
Is it offering the features
that my online customer wants?

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:34.070
LinkedIn a long time ago,
really got started this way.

00:37:34.070 --> 00:37:35.840
They continue to operate that way.

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:38.980
Think about the gamification you have,
and I use LinkedIn as a professional.

00:37:38.980 --> 00:37:41.720
Most of us are familiar
with that social platform.

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:44.680
Think about the gamification that
goes on constantly with them

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:48.730
telling us how we can improve our profile,
how much further we can go along.

00:37:48.730 --> 00:37:52.290
You've seen a lot of the companies
that appeal out to developers,

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.870
like the Twilio's of the world,
are using a level

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:58.860
of growth hacking in what they're
doing to drive those conversions.

00:37:58.860 --> 00:38:02.798
Actually when you look back, one of
the original slides that I had shared.

00:38:02.798 --> 00:38:07.477
Dropbox, Zynga, Groupon,
Instagram, Pinterest, Twitter,

00:38:07.477 --> 00:38:12.200
Facebook, Evernote, AppSumo,
Stumbleupon, Everlane.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:15.685
All of these companies have been
very active in using the growth

00:38:15.685 --> 00:38:16.450
hacking construct.

00:38:18.560 --> 00:38:19.060
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:24.140
So another question we have here is, what
in your option, is the best practice for

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:28.540
a start-up to build
a culture of growth hacking?

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:30.870
&gt;&gt; That is an awesome question.

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:32.010
First get to your CEO really fast, or

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.280
if you're the founder,
get your head around it.

00:38:35.380 --> 00:38:36.680
It really does start top down.

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:40.150
And so I think it really comes down
to sitting down with the founders,

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:43.840
sitting down with the CEO and
really thinking about,

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:48.730
when we look at our approach to
how we're going to drive growth.

00:38:48.730 --> 00:38:52.880
Do we see this is something
that we want marketing driven?

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:56.890
And if so, then really started
to lay out and I had listed the,

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:58.130
kind of, core tenants.

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:00.130
Getting everyone's heads around that.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:02.240
How are we going to use the product for
this?

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:07.010
How is the head of growth involved and
able to really drive and

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:09.108
touch all the disciplines
to make this happen?

00:39:09.108 --> 00:39:13.110
So, I think the way I would approach
it if I were coming into a company,

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:18.300
I would say, sit down with the top
management say, is this right for us?

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:22.490
Understand and listen to
the reasons why it may or may not.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:25.610
And then really, working with
the management team to say this is

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:27.950
what it's going to take for
us to really execute on this.

00:39:27.950 --> 00:39:29.820
So it's gotta be top down.

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:32.560
The hard part would be,
if you went it, bottoms up.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:35.440
I've just been hired as the new CMO,
the head of marketing of this startup.

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.550
And I'm going to go do growth hacking.

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:41.953
And no one from the top is acknowledging
that this is how we're going to drive

00:39:41.953 --> 00:39:46.866
growth in the company, and then you'll
just constantly be hitting brick walls.

00:39:46.866 --> 00:39:51.693
&gt;&gt; Okay, so we have another question
here that says, traditionally,

00:39:51.693 --> 00:39:56.785
marketing takes a long view on bad news or
bad mentions in popular media.

00:39:56.785 --> 00:39:59.157
Because eventually, it'll be forgotten and

00:39:59.157 --> 00:40:03.420
name recognition benefits eventually
outweigh the short-term losses.

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:05.570
Does this concept hold true
on growth hacking as well?

00:40:05.570 --> 00:40:08.778
&gt;&gt; I would say, not, and I would also,
from my own perspective, and

00:40:08.778 --> 00:40:12.559
I've worked both in the startup world,
as well as very large companies, so,

00:40:12.559 --> 00:40:15.683
I'm really familiar with what
you're putting forward there.

00:40:15.683 --> 00:40:18.812
I would say,
because of the advent of social media,

00:40:18.812 --> 00:40:22.725
that the reality is all companies,
growth hacking companies and

00:40:22.725 --> 00:40:27.950
traditional marketing companies, are
acting much more quickly across bad news.

00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:29.413
Because you just can't
let it fester up there.

00:40:29.413 --> 00:40:32.430
Now, they might have different strategies
about how they're going to do it.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:33.771
But it's very hard just
to let things fester.

00:40:33.771 --> 00:40:36.194
because they just don't go
away the way they used to.

00:40:36.194 --> 00:40:39.976
I can tap into my own career without
giving specifics and say, there are so

00:40:39.976 --> 00:40:44.190
many times that I've seen where maybe
there was one bad review, one bad comment.

00:40:45.338 --> 00:40:47.429
Wouldn't you not,
every time you do a Google search,

00:40:47.429 --> 00:40:48.528
it comes back up on page one.

00:40:48.528 --> 00:40:50.640
I wish I could bury it.

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:51.988
But I can't get it off the radar.

00:40:51.988 --> 00:40:54.900
So, I think all companies now,
quite frankly,

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:57.102
are having to take a more proactive,

00:40:57.102 --> 00:41:01.663
a more rapid fire approach to how they
handle the bad news that's out there.

00:41:01.663 --> 00:41:05.210
Now, putting that aside, knowing that
there's some very large traditional

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:07.748
organizations that are a little
slower to move on that.

00:41:07.748 --> 00:41:12.332
In a growth hacking world now, you gotta
constantly be reacting, responding.

00:41:12.332 --> 00:41:15.010
You don't just want to wait for
something to go away.

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:17.412
I think also in the companies
I've watched and

00:41:17.412 --> 00:41:20.492
companies I've been in that
are applying growth hacking,

00:41:20.492 --> 00:41:24.945
there's much more transparency in terms
of interacting with that target audience.

00:41:24.945 --> 00:41:29.149
And I think also there's a new bar
that as we look at millennials and

00:41:29.149 --> 00:41:32.730
younger Gen Xers versus Gen Xers and
baby boomers.

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:36.330
There's a different bar that's also being
given to us by our target audience that

00:41:36.330 --> 00:41:38.410
says, hey, be transparent, be rapid.

00:41:38.410 --> 00:41:40.858
Make sure you're connecting
with me on good and the bad or

00:41:40.858 --> 00:41:42.083
I'm going somewhere else.

00:41:42.083 --> 00:41:46.670
So, I think there's major shifts that's
going on now in the world of marketing.

00:41:46.670 --> 00:41:51.310
And definitely, in growth hacking, [LAUGH]
you address things fast, you move, and

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:52.240
you get onto the next thing.

00:41:53.980 --> 00:41:55.210
Love that question.

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:56.446
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Good question.

00:41:56.446 --> 00:42:03.140
So, we have a few people actually asking
something relating to how the role

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:08.260
of a product and marketing teams
are now changed with the growth hacker.

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:14.700
And how are they thinking about growth
together and how they play a part?

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:19.737
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and so, this comes back to that
company culture model I was talking about.

00:42:19.737 --> 00:42:24.993
In a more traditional environment, what I
would say is if there was a product issue,

00:42:24.993 --> 00:42:27.805
if you had noticed as
the head of marketing and

00:42:27.805 --> 00:42:30.185
you didn't own product management.

00:42:30.185 --> 00:42:34.498
So, if you notice that there's a product
issue that is preventing you from moving

00:42:34.498 --> 00:42:38.124
forward, getting more users,
having customers who are negative,

00:42:38.124 --> 00:42:42.396
you would really have to sit down with
your peer and have this discussion around.

00:42:42.396 --> 00:42:45.730
Hey, I see these problems,
I'd like to share them with you.

00:42:45.730 --> 00:42:48.060
Let's work together to find a solution.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:50.310
And depending on the road map,

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:53.540
depending on the process that was
being used on product development.

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:56.630
It may or
may not happen in a period of time.

00:42:56.630 --> 00:43:02.200
In the Growth Hacking Model,
the pure, true Growth Hacking Model,

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:04.590
I wouldn't have to have that
side-by-side conversation.

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:08.760
Instead, I would be empowered
to go to engineering, or

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:13.980
go to the product management team,
and say, all right, you know,

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:17.080
we've lost 15% on our
conversion rates because of x.

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:19.630
When are you going to have it changed?

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:22.881
So, there's a different mentality
there from a cultural perspective and

00:43:22.881 --> 00:43:24.952
what you own if you're
in real growth hacking.

00:43:24.952 --> 00:43:28.098
Now, companies will do hybrid models.

00:43:28.098 --> 00:43:31.850
It's essentially one of
the companies I'm working with now,

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:34.350
we have that hybrid model.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:38.630
The way we've all been at there to make
sure that we get what we need to change

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:41.760
in the product is it really
comes down to everyone

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.700
understands that growth is
the number one objective.

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:46.760
And everyone's into it saying, all right,

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:48.460
we're all going to look
at the numbers every day.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.303
You're responsible for them and
driving them at the head of growth.

00:43:51.303 --> 00:43:54.218
But we're all going to be looking
at those numbers each day and

00:43:54.218 --> 00:43:57.831
you find that when you create this
culture of, hey, it's about growth,

00:43:57.831 --> 00:43:59.597
that's our number one objective.

00:43:59.597 --> 00:44:02.675
Even if you don't own it,
you can't go and say, engineering do it,

00:44:02.675 --> 00:44:05.330
because everyone gets that
that's the fundamental goal,

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:07.737
you'll find a lot more
willingness to be a part of it.

00:44:07.737 --> 00:44:10.787
And then, it's on your shoulders
to produce the reports,

00:44:10.787 --> 00:44:14.325
put up the bright shining panels,
have monitors that are constantly

00:44:14.325 --> 00:44:18.061
showing the numbers to get the culture
thinking about a growth culture.

00:44:18.061 --> 00:44:23.490
&gt;&gt; Great, so, I think we have time for
one more question.

00:44:23.490 --> 00:44:26.580
So, we have a lot of people asking.

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:31.915
You talked a lot about some of the tools
and strategies for a growth hacker.

00:44:31.915 --> 00:44:35.320
But in your opinion,
which are the most effective?

00:44:35.320 --> 00:44:38.754
If any, which are the ones that
we need to focus on in order to

00:44:38.754 --> 00:44:40.286
be a great growth hacker?

00:44:40.286 --> 00:44:41.333
&gt;&gt; Yeah, good question.

00:44:41.333 --> 00:44:46.310
So, first of all, let's say, it always
comes back to who your target audience is.

00:44:46.310 --> 00:44:49.607
And whenever I look at doing anything
at marketing, I always come back and

00:44:49.607 --> 00:44:50.413
I call it the who.

00:44:50.413 --> 00:44:53.650
I always come back to try and
figure out who's my target audience.

00:44:53.650 --> 00:44:56.600
And I ask questions like,
how did they learn?

00:44:56.600 --> 00:44:58.060
Where do they live?

00:44:58.060 --> 00:45:00.790
What is their temperament
towards marketing?

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:05.633
And I try to really get in their
shoes because any tactic you deploy.

00:45:05.633 --> 00:45:09.492
It may be a great tactic, but
if it isn't in line with how your target

00:45:09.492 --> 00:45:13.100
audience thinks about the world,
it's not going to go very far.

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:16.470
So, I would first say,
think about who your target audience is.

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:18.430
Really do that sub-segmentation.

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:20.310
Really get into their shoes.

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:24.039
Really understand that individual
you're trying to cater to.

00:45:24.039 --> 00:45:26.335
And out of that will fall the tactics.

00:45:26.335 --> 00:45:31.344
Now, from a growth hacking perspective,
what I would say is it is the end product.

00:45:31.344 --> 00:45:35.973
If you have that opportunity because you
have an online product and you are using

00:45:35.973 --> 00:45:40.603
your website as the main way that you're
driving growth, it's going to be really

00:45:40.603 --> 00:45:45.330
looking for what can you do in product
that will spawn other people's interest.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:48.250
So, it goes back to really that
whole list of in products tactics.

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:51.930
And again, it's very specialized to
what your product or your service is.

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:55.470
But again, think about your consumer,
think about your customer.

00:45:55.470 --> 00:45:58.947
And by the way, this can be done
in the B2B world or the B2C world.

00:45:58.947 --> 00:45:59.993
Get in their shoes, and

00:45:59.993 --> 00:46:02.830
that will drive which of those
tactics are going to be most useful.

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:05.281
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Lynda.

00:46:05.281 --> 00:46:10.005
Great, and also, thank all of you for
attending this webinar.

00:46:10.005 --> 00:46:13.015
I hope you learned something
new about growth hacking.

00:46:13.015 --> 00:46:16.845
And we look forward to seeing you
next time in the next webinar.

00:46:16.845 --> 00:46:19.960
And if you want more information,
please contact us or

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:22.570
look at our website at
create.stanford.edu.

00:46:22.570 --> 00:46:24.665
Thank you everyone.

